{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791556261,"nonce":"vVvApLVa0WBqtLktaCzsiX/nVNE=","message":"Bare T analysis (2026-10-09, nuc probe ~/probes/pbare): a generic body with an unconstrained T is lowered ONCE with T erased to i64 (`fn @Main.isNullCall($0: i64)`), and callers pass the raw scalar (`const 0i32`). `value == null` lowers to haxe_reflect_compare_typed(value, null, 0), which sees 0 vs 0. Inside the shared body a zero Int, 0.0 and false are indistinguishable from null, so no comparison-site fix can work. Not monomorphized: the MIR monomorphizer only instantiates functions lowered with TypeVar params. 'inline' does not help either: isNull(0) is still a call (fn2) in MIR. Options: (a) box scalars at bare-T call boundaries -- Haxe's own static-target semantics, but changes the erased-T ABI everywhere and box rules keyed this way have lost rows before; (b) specialize generic functions whose body compares a bare T with null, per scalar instantiation, folding T==null to false; (c) inline such bodies at TAST/HIR with T substituted, so 0 == null folds. (b) is the narrowest correct one. Probe output now: inline/call both 'true true true true false' vs Haxe 'false false false true false'.","files":null}]}